-- Schema migration: version 1 → 2.
--
-- Intentionally a no-op DDL (PRAGMA user_version assignment). Earlier this
-- file used `SELECT 1;` as the no-op marker, but AndroidSqliteDriver routes
-- migration statements through `executeForChangedRowCount` (it expects a
-- write); SELECT throws SQLiteException("Queries can be performed using
-- SQLiteDatabase query or rawQuery methods only") and crashes the upgrade.
-- A PRAGMA write is the cheapest valid statement that doesn't require any
-- table or column to exist — `user_version` is set by the framework anyway,
-- so re-asserting it has zero side-effect on the on-disk schema.
--
-- Why a no-op rather than no file at all: with no `.sqm` present, schema
-- version stays at 1 forever, and the day someone needs migration #2 they
-- discover the verifyMigrations / Schema.migrate plumbing has never run in
-- practice. Better to exercise the path now under a no-op than under the
-- pressure of a real schema change.
--
-- File-naming convention reminder for the next migration:
--   • <N>.sqm migrates FROM schema version N TO version N+1.
--   • So this file (1.sqm) bumps version 1 → 2; the next bump (2 → 3) is
--     2.sqm; etc. SQLDelight runs them in order on first open of an older
--     DB file (`AndroidSqliteDriver.Callback.onUpgrade`).
--   • verifyMigrations = true (build.gradle.kts) round-trips
--     schema → migration → schema and fails the build if they diverge.

PRAGMA user_version = 2;
